Two traps that cost a build each, both about `rw` vs `simp` on schematic lemmas: (1) `rw [Bool.true_and]` (or any lemma with a metavariable, e.g. `a && true`/`true && a`) only rewrites the FIRST unifying occurrence it finds, not every occurrence of the pattern with different instantiations -- so `rw [h1, Bool.true_and, hb, hbn]` silently only fixed the x-side copy and left the y-side `true && y.testBit (...)` untouched, three errors downstream. Fix: `simp only [...]` for anything with a metavariable (it rewrites everywhere); reserve `rw` for fully closed/concrete equalities like `show n + 2 - 2 = n by omega`, which ARE textually the same term on both the x-side and y-side subterms and so `rw` does hit both at once. (2) `simp` will silently renormalize a Nat subtraction expression like `n + 1 - 2` to `n - 1` on its own before a `have e2 : x.testBit (n + 1 - 2) = ...` gets a chance to match, producing an "unused simp arg" warning and leaving the goal one level deeper than expected (with an extra `decide (1 ≤ n) || !x.testBit (n - 1)` shape from simp unfolding testBit_rowStep's `i - 1`-referencing subterm further because n+1-2 collapsed to n-1 which is small enough for simp to keep unfolding). Fix: `rw [show n + 1 - 2 = n - 1 by omega]` explicitly to the SAME normal form simp would reach, and state the `have` in that already-normalized form (`x.testBit (n - 1) = y.testBit (n - 1)`), so it lands as a literal simp-set hit rather than racing simp's own arithmetic normalization.

For hlt (bits < n+1), the working by_cases order is OUTER on `2 ≤ i`, not on `1 ≤ i`: in the innermost false/false branch you need BOTH `¬2≤i` and `¬1≤i` visible to `simp` to collapse both `decide` terms to `false` -- splitting on `1 ≤ i` first and only introducing `2 ≤ i` inside the true branch leaves the false/outer branch (i=0) with no `h2` in scope and an unsolved `decide (2 ≤ i) && x.testBit (i-2) = decide (2 ≤ i) && y.testBit (i-2)` goal that `simp [h1, e0]` alone cannot close.","journal":"Proved rowStep_agree_succ_two_iff. The shape mirrors the closed rowNat_return_succ_iff/rowNat_return_succ_two pair almost exactly (same testBit_rowStep unfolding, same private helpers), but stripped down to plain naturals x, y instead of rowNat T, rowNat (T+p) — nothing about the picture is needed, just Nat.testBit arithmetic. Two build iterations, both from the same class of Lean gotcha: `rw` with a lemma that has a free metavariable (like `Bool.true_and : true && a = a`) only fixes the first place it matches, not every place — looks like it worked, then leaves the mirror-image copy (the y-side of an x/y symmetric goal) untouched three lines later. Wrote it up in the notebook since it's bitten this project's provers before under other names and is worth a project-wide callout if it keeps happening."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":238,"cache_read_input_tokens":161975,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":238},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fcd6d377-83bc-4c16-bfef-62f7ca3491b8","uuid":"368249dc-a1bc-498e-93a6-767db7d55c30","timestamp":"2026-09-10T20:25:31.825Z","request_id":"req_011CevPKQrs3mxSLak4hZYDS"}

